I had to modify this problem to ask for an exact solution instead of a decimal approximation to stump the models: 9/10 of the Gemini models returned the correct 6-digit decimal approximation to the series in question. I also examined the logical steps these models were returning. Largely, the AI models understand the problem well enough to find an Euler product for the multiplicative $\\sum_n f(n)n^\{-s\}$. The gap is that the models can only return the requested DGF series by computing the $n$ such that $\\omega(n), \\Omega(n) \\equiv 0 \\pmod\{2\}$ and then forming the corresponding DGF by truncating the full sum up to 5 decimal digits of accuracy by summing over a finite range of $n \\geq 1$ satisfying $f(n) n^\{-11/2\} < 10^\{-6\}$. It is good that the models understand part of the problem. However, it is clear from the outputs that these models do not deeply understand a notion of what makes a DGF of $f(n) n^\{-s\}$ over all positive integers $n$ with $\\omega(n), \\Omega(n)$ both of even parity. My way of verifying this was to then modify my original problem\'92s answer format in such a way that the requested series value has to be expressed exactly - in this case in terms of $s := 11/2$, the prime zeta function $P(s) := \\sum_p p^\{-s\}$ for $\\operatorname\{Re\}(s) > 1$ and other standard mathematical functions and constants.\
\
